UID:09-00-t0-keynote-boldly-go-building-worlds-room-on-bridge@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:Boldly Go, Building Worlds: Is there Room for me on the Bridge?
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Dawn Wages\n\nStar Trek made a promise to me that I grew up believing: the future would have room for everyone, and that the team we need to face that uncertain future together will look different from each other. Octavia Butler added color and friction to that promise, sharing a future that was written with specificity and honesty; and Black woman led. Speculative fiction, scifi and Afrofuturism are what first let me imagine myself in rooms I hadn't been invited into yet — and it shaped the aesthetic and the optimism and agency I still bring to my work today.
 I've spent a decade plus building things in the Python community: as a conference organizer, maintainer, speaker, Chair of the Python Software Foundation Board, people manager, entrepreneur. Along the way I've learned that building a better future isn't just about imagining it. It's about doing the work in public, making room for the people who weren't handed a seat at the table, and staying accountable to the crew you're actually flying w...
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/keynote-monday/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260824T102000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260824T110500
DTSTAMP:20260824T102000Z
UID:10-20-t0-good-conduct-how-django-modernized-its-code-of-conduct@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:Good Conduct: How Django Modernized Its Code of Conduct
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Dan Ryan\n\nA Code of Conduct is only as strong as the community's trust in it. When it's time to revise one, the how matters as much as the what. Django's Working Group undertook a significant revision in 2026 — adopting CC3's expanded encouraged and restricted behaviors, adding explicit guidance on consent and impact versus intent, formalizing a graduated enforcement ladder, and even addressing the responsible use of AI-generated content. This was not just a find-and-replace; it required community input, DSF Board approval, careful sourcing from the PSF, OpenJS Foundation, Mozilla, and others, and an updated, transparency-first documentation process.
 The talk opens with a look at what makes Contributor Covenant 3 meaningfully different from earlier versions — the shift toward encouraging positive behaviors, stronger language around impact versus intent, and explicit attention to consent and boundaries. From there, it digs into what Django's update actually changed in practice: new sections o...
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/good-conduct-how-django-modernized-its-code-of-conduct/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260824T111000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260824T113500
DTSTAMP:20260824T111000Z
UID:11-10-t0-geodjango-at-city-scale-spatial-data-for-urban-systems@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:GeoDjango at City Scale: Spatial Data for Urban Systems
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Drishti Jain\n\nIn this talk, I’ll delve into how GeoDjango can be used to build scalable spatial data platforms for smart cities and urban systems. Cities are fundamentally spatial systems—roads, buildings, utilities, mobility flows, and environmental sensors all exist in geographic space—and modeling these systems requires tools that can handle geospatial data at scale. GeoDjango, combined with PostGIS, provides a powerful framework for representing, querying, and visualizing this spatial information in web applications.
 I’ll begin by exploring how urban entities such as buildings, transit routes, sensor locations, and zoning boundaries can be modeled using GeoDjango’s spatial fields and ORM abstractions. Attendees will see how spatial indexes and geospatial queries enable proximity search, containment checks, routing queries, and large-scale spatial analytics directly from Django applications.
 Next, I’ll examine architectural and performance considerations for city-scale spatial systems, inclu...
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/geodjango-at-city-scale-spatial-data-for-urban-systems/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260824T114000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260824T120500
DTSTAMP:20260824T114000Z
UID:11-40-t0-pragmatic-ai-how-to-gain-trust-with-user-centered-ai-adoption@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:Pragmatic AI: How to gain trust with user-centered AI adoption
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Meagen Voss\n\nWith some CEOs of large AI companies claiming AI cures for cancer are just around the corner, you might feel like you’re a fool for not integrating AI right away in your software. This talk will show you why resisting that pressure can be a good thing and how taking a steady and thoughtful approach to AI adoption in your open source project can help you stand out.
 At the Wagtail CMS project, we recognized the transformative potential of AI tools but we also observed that rushed AI adoption has led to poor implementations, costly mistakes, and user backlash. Not to mention, many people understandably have environmental and ethical concerns about these technologies.
 I’ll show you:
 
 The importance of creating guiding principles for AI adoption
 Why we decided against incorporating AI in our core product
 How we designed our Wagtail AI package to give our users agency and choice
 
 I’ll demonstrate how our approach has built loyalty and goodwill among existing users as well as how i...
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/pragmatic-ai-how-to-gain-trust-with-user-centered-ai-adoption/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260824T121000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260824T130000
