UID:09-05-to-keynote-emails-from-my-grandad@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:Keynote: Emails from my Grandad
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Sarah Boyce\n\nCode review can be a painful and anxiety-inducing experience. As developers, our work is regularly scrutinized by our colleagues. Comments about bugs, edge cases, or “code smells” are a normal part of the process, but when you’re the author, it can sometimes feel like you’re under attack.
 I learned at a young age that my grandad would light up if I involved him in my work. When I told him about a presentation I was preparing, a few days later an email would arrive (often with a Word document attached titled something like “Sarah ideas” or “Sarah extra ideas”). Inside were pages of suggestions. Sometimes they were small. Sometimes they were big...
 And yet, through those emails, I always felt something else: love.
 In this talk I’ll explore how we receive feedback and our relationship with it. Drawing on lessons from those emails and experiences in code review and mentorship, I hope to invite you to reconsider criticism, not as an attack, but as a gift.
 
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/keynote-tuesday-emails-from-my-grandad/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260825T102000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260825T110500
DTSTAMP:20260825T102000Z
UID:10-20-t0-batteries-vs-speed-the-django-fastapi-debate@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:Batteries vs. Speed: The Django/FastAPI Debate
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Calvin Hendryx-Parker\nPresenter: Frank Wiles\n\nFew debates in the Python world generate more discussion than Django vs. FastAPI. After decades of building production systems with both, we've arrived at a simple conclusion: they don't actually do the same thing, and that changes everything about how you should evaluate them.
 This talk walks through a structured, no-hype comparison across three areas:
 What they share:
 
 Python-native development experience
 Async support
 Ecosystem depth and active communities
 
 Where Django excels:
 
 Batteries-included philosophy
 The ORM, admin, auth, and forms out of the box
 A rich plugin ecosystem that makes full-stack web development fast and predictable
 
 Where FastAPI pulls ahead:
 
 Blazing performance
 First-class type hints
 A lightweight footprint ideal for shipping a focused API layer fast
 
 Co-presented by two custom software development agency leaders who have made this exact call on behalf of real clients, this talk won't crown a winner. Instead, it will leave you with a pra...
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/batteries-vs-speed-the-django-fastapi-debate/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260825T111000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260825T113500
DTSTAMP:20260825T111000Z
UID:11-10-t0-the-testing-pyramid-in-practice-for-django@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:The Testing Pyramid in Practice for Django
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Monica Oyugi\n\nMany Django developers write tests reactively — covering whatever feels important in the moment and end up with suites that are slow, hard to maintain, and unevenly distributed. This talk addresses that by introducing the testing pyramid as a deliberate, structured approach, anchored in Django's latest testing capabilities.
 
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/the-testing-pyramid-in-practice-for-django/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
