UID:13-50-t0-who-goes-there-actively-detecting-intruders-with-cyber-deception-tools@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:Who Goes There? Actively Detecting Intruders With Cyber Deception Tools
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Dwayne McDaniel\n\nThe best time to detect an attacker is while they are still inside your environment, before stolen data, lateral movement, or privilege escalation turns a foothold into a breach. Unfortunately, many organizations still learn what happened only after the damage is done.
 Attackers follow patterns, and one of the clearest is credential hunting. After gaining access, they search for hardcoded secrets, API keys, tokens, and other credentials that can help them expand control. That behavior creates an opportunity for defenders.
 This talk explores honeytokens, lightweight deceptive assets designed to look like valuable credentials but exist only to alert defenders when an attacker interacts with them. Unlike full honeypot environments, honeytokens are easy to deploy, require minimal maintenance, and can be embedded where attackers are most likely to look.
 Attendees will learn how honeytokens fit into a modern detection strategy, why they work so well against credential-driven attacks, a...
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/who-goes-there-actively-detecting-intruders-with-cyber-deception-tools/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260825T144000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260825T150500
DTSTAMP:20260825T144000Z
UID:14-40-t0-polyglot-persistence-with-django-when-one-database-isn-t-enough@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:Polyglot Persistence with Django: When One Database Isn't Enough
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Abigail Afi Gbadago\n\nPolyglot persistence is the use of multiple databases in a single application, with each chosen for what it does best. Django ships with multi-database support: define your connections, write a router, and in theory, you're done. In practice, the gap between &quot;Django supports this&quot; and &quot;I have this working&quot; is where most developers get stuck. This talk closes that gap by using a Django MongoDB backend alongside PostgreSQL, with honest coverage of where the ORM abstraction leaks, what migrations look like against a document store, and lessons learned from building with MongoDB's Queryable Encryption in a real Django context. Attendees leave with working patterns, a clear decision framework, and an answer to whether polyglot persistence is the best choice for their project.
 
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/polyglot-persistence-with-django-when-one-database-isn-t-enough/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260825T154000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260825T162500
DTSTAMP:20260825T154000Z
UID:15-40-t0-50-shades-of-green-one-contribution-to-the-django-world@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:50 shades of green - One contribution to the Django world
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Sarah Abderemane\n\nDjango's website has a dark theme. Many developers around the world use it every day. Some never even noticed it appear. Others switched immediately and never looked back.
 Either way, there's a story behind it.
 This talk walks through the complete arc of that contribution: the motivation, the implementation details, the review process, and what it meant to get it across the finish line on one of the most widely used frameworks in the Python ecosystem.
 A practical and personal look at open source contribution: what it takes, what you learn, and why it matters more than you might think.
 
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/50-shades-of-green-one-contribution-to-the-django-world/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260825T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260825T165500
DTSTAMP:20260825T163000Z
UID:16-30-t0-what-s-new-in-postgres-18-19@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:What's New in Postgres 18 & 19
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Elizabeth Christensen\n\nThis session will cover in detail new features of the software and how they affect your database/Django work. I’ll give some advice on what to take advantage of right away and what to maybe let the other guys try first.
 
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/what-s-new-in-postgres-18-19/
