UID:10-20-t0-scaling-django-mastering-database-routers-with-aws-aurora@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:Scaling Django: Mastering Database Routers with AWS Aurora
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Leonardo Batista\n\nAs your Django application grows, the database is often the first major bottleneck. But how do you scale horizontally without introducing massive complexity into your application logic? In this talk, we’ll explore how OneBlinc leveraged Django's native Database Routers to distribute load across an AWS Aurora cluster. We will go beyond the documentation to discuss real-world implementation: managing read/write splitting, handling replication lag, ensuring data consistency in financial transactions. If you want to squeeze every bit of performance out of your cloud database while keeping your Django code clean, this session is for you.
 
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/scaling-django-mastering-database-routers-with-aws-aurora/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260826T111000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260826T113500
DTSTAMP:20260826T111000Z
UID:11-10-t0-auto-prefetching-with-model-field-fetch-modes-in-django-6-1@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:Auto-prefetching with model field fetch modes in Django 6.1
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Jacob Walls\n\nNew in Django 6.1, model field fetch modes provide a way to avoid the deluge of queries from accessing un-fetched objects during a loop (also known as the &quot;N+1 queries&quot; problem). You may be familiar with how Django's prefetch_related() and select_related() methods can prevent these queries, but unlike those tools, the &quot;fetch peers&quot; mode does not require maintaining a list of fields to fetch. This can significantly improve project maintenance, especially when QuerySets are constructed at some distance from where they are used.
 In this talk, we'll cover what's new about fetch modes, what tradeoffs are entailed, how to configure fetch modes per-model via managers, and how to use the &quot;raise&quot; mode to surface issues in development or put guardrails around performance-intensive sections of code.
 
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/auto-prefetching-with-model-field-fetch-modes-in-django-6-1/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260826T114000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260826T120500
DTSTAMP:20260826T114000Z
UID:11-40-t0-enter-the-ecosystem-contributing-to-django-open-source-projects@https://2026.djangocon.us
SUMMARY:Enter the Ecosystem: Contributing to Django Open Source Projects
LOCATION:Sauganash Ballroom
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Andrew Selzer\n\nTaking that first step into open source can feel overwhelming, but it doesn't have to be. This talk is a friendly, practical guide for developers who are curious about contributing to projects in the Django ecosystem but haven't made that first contribution yet.
 We'll start by asking yourself why you want to contribute in the first place. Having a clear goal will keep you motivated when you hit those inevitable roadblocks. From there, we'll walk through how to find a project that aligns with your interests, skills, and availability, how to take the time to understand a project's contribution process, and how to find the right first issue, whether that's a code change, a documentation update, a bug report, or a code review.
 We'll also cover what happens after you submit your work, how to receive feedback with an open mind, iterate, and celebrate getting that first pull request or change merged. We'll also touch on how to use AI tools responsibly as part of your workflow.
 You'll le...
URL:https://2026.djangocon.us/talks/enter-the-ecosystem-contributing-to-django-open-source-projects/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
